another approach to construct modular polynomials of higher level purely algebraically. In particular, we show the existence of modular polynomials for invariants directly related to models of elliptic curves, such as the coefficients of Montgomery and Hessian curves. We also show that these modular polynomials have integer coefficients and are symmetric and irreducible in certain cases, and give an algorithm to compute them, which is based on the deformation method by Kunzweiler and Robert.\n\nView Paper\n\n6 views\n\nv1\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{onuki2026geometricconstructionmodularpolynomials, title={Geometric construction of modular polynomials with level structures}, author={Hiroshi Onuki and Yukihiro Uchida and Ryo Yoshizumi}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.17338}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.NT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17338}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Extending Type $B$ Parking Spaces | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2601.18090)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{adams2026extendingtypebparking, title={On Extending Type $B$ Parking Spaces}, author={Anthony Adams and Joshua Dorsam and Lily Levitsky and Megan Mann}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.18090}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.18090}, }\nSubmitted 26 Jan 2026\n\n# On Extending Type $B$B Parking Spaces\n\nAA\n\nAnthony Adams\n\nJD\n\nJoshua Dorsam\n\nLL\n\nLily Levitsky\n\nMM\n\nMegan Mann\n\n## Abstract\n\nArmstrong, Reiner, and Rhoades defined for all Weyl groups $W$W a natural representation of $W$W called the $W$W-parking space. The type $B$B parking space is the representation $\\mathbb{C}[(\\mathbb{Z}/(2n+1)\\mathbb{Z})^n]$C[(Z/(2 n+1)Z)n] of the $n$n th signed symmetric group. We consider more general representations of the form $\\mathbb{C}[(\\mathbb{Z}/m\\mathbb{Z})^n]$C[(Z/m Z)n]; we conjecture that this representation extends to the $(n+1)$(n+1)th signed symmetric group for all $n$n and $m$m. We prove this conjecture when $m = 3$m=3 or when $n \\leq 2$n≤2.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{adams2026extendingtypebparking, title={On Extending Type $B$ Parking Spaces}, author={Anthony Adams and Joshua Dorsam and Lily Levitsky and Megan Mann}, year={2026}, eprint={2601.18090}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.18090}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.17338] Geometric construction of modular polynomials with level structures (https://slimjectvitamins.com/accel-vitamin-b-complex-capsules/?_=%2Fabs%2F2601.17338%23CD3DgejCa2b3OIySVEszRQc%3D)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Cite as:  | arXiv:2601.17338 [math.NT]\n\n# Mathematics > Number Theory\n\n[Submitted on 24 Jan 2026]\n\n# Title:Geometric construction of modular polynomials with level structures\n\nAuthors:Hiroshi Onuki, Yukihiro Uchida, Ryo Yoshizumi\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Geometric construction of modular polynomials with level structures, by Hiroshi Onuki and 2 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:The classical modular polynomial for $j$-invariants describes the relation between two elliptic curves connected by isogenies. This polynomial has been applied to various algorithms in computational number theory, such as point counting on elliptic curves. In addition, computing the modular polynomial itself is also an important problem, and various algorithms to compute it have been proposed. On the other hand, modular polynomials for other invariants of higher level structures have also been studied. For example, the modular polynomials for the Legendre $\\lambda$-invariant and the Weber functions are well-known. In this paper, we give another approach to construct modular polynomials of higher level purely algebraically. In particular, we show the existence of modular polynomials for invariants directly related to models of elliptic curves, such as the coefficients of Montgomery and Hessian curves. We also show that these modular polynomials have integer coefficients and are symmetric and irreducible in certain cases, and give an algorithm to compute them, which is based on the deformation method by Kunzweiler and Robert.\n\nSubjects:  | Number Theory (math.NT)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2601.17338 [math.NT]\n   | (or arXiv:2601.17338v1 [math.NT] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.17338 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Hiroshi Onuki [view email]\n[v1] Sat, 24 Jan 2026 06:53:40 UTC (29 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDegree-choosability of proper conflict-free list coloring of sparse graphs | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/400003342_Degree-choosability_of_proper_conflict-free_list_coloring_of_sparse_graphs)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2601.15611\n\n# Degree-choosability of proper conflict-free list coloring of sparse graphs\n\n  * January 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2601.15611\n\nAuthors:\n\nMasaki Kashima\n\nMasaki Kashima\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nRiste Škrekovski\n\n  * University of Ljubljana\n\nRongxing Xu\n\n  * Zhejiang Normal University\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a graph G and a mapping $f:V(G) \\to \\mathbb{N}$, an f-list assignment of G is a function that maps each $v \\in V(G)$ to a set of at least f(v) colors. For an f-list assignment L of a graph G, a proper conflict-free L-coloring of G is a proper coloring ϕ of G such that for every vertex $v \\in V(G)$, $ϕ(v) \\in L(v)$ and some appears precisely once in the neighborhood of v. We say that G is proper conflict-free f-choosable if for every f-list assignment L of G, there exists a proper conflict-free L-coloring of G. If G is proper conflict-free f-choosable and there is a constant k such that $f(v)= d_G(v)+k$ for every vertex v of G, then we say G is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+k)$-choosable. In this paper, we consider graphs with a bounded maximum average degree. We show that every graph with the maximum average degree less than $\\frac{10}{3}$ is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+3)$-choosable, and that every graph with the maximum average degree less than $\\frac{18}{7}$ is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+2)$-choosable. As a result, every planar graph with girth at least 5 is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+3)$-choosable, and every planar graph with girth at least 9 is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+2)$-choosable.\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (12)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nRemarks on proper conflict-free colorings of graphs\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Mar 2022\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Yair Caro\n  * Mirko Petruševski\n  * Riste Škrekovski\n\nDiscrete Mathematics Volume 346, Issue 2, February 2023, 113221 A vertex coloring of a graph is said to be conflict-free with respect to neighborhoods if for every non-isolated vertex there is a color appearing exactly once in its (open) neighborhood. We also establish tight upper bounds for outerplanar graphs.\n\nView\n\nWe define a list assignment L * of V (G) \\ V (G ′ ) as follows: •\n\n  * G Thus\n\nThus G ′ has no isolated vertices. For each v ∈ {u k, v 1,..., v k−1 }, let c v be a color in U ϕ (v, G ′ ). We define a list assignment L * of V (G) \\ V (G ′ ) as follows: • Let L * (u 0 ) = L(u 0 ) \\ {ϕ(u k ), c u k }.\n\nNote that two neighbors of v 3 in G ′ have distinct colors. For each v ∈ {u 4 , w 1 , w 2 }, let c v be a color in U ϕ (v, G ′ )\n\n  * G Thus\n\nThus, G ′ has no isolated vertices. By Proposition 3.1 and the minimality of G, G ′ admits a proper conflict-free L-coloring ϕ. Note that two neighbor