r all $n$ and $m$. We prove this conjecture when $m = 3$ or when $n \\leq 2$.\n\nSubject: Combinatorics\n\nPublish: 2026-01-26 03:00:59 UTC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDegree-plus-one conjecture for graphs of minimum degree three | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/372692/degree-plus-one-conjecture-for-graphs-of-minimum-degree-thre)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Source: Masaki Kashima, Riste Škrekovski and Rongxing Xu, “Degree-choosability of proper conflict-free list coloring of sparse graphs”, arXiv:2601.15611 (2026).\n\n# Degree-plus-one conjecture for graphs of minimum degree three\n\n1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nFor a graph $G$G, write $d_G(v)$d G​(v) for the degree of a vertex $v$v. A $({\\rm degree}+1)$(degree+1)-list assignment assigns each vertex $v$v a list $L(v)$L(v) with $|L(v)|\\geq d_G(v)+1$∣L(v)∣≥d G​(v)+1. A graph is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+1)$(degree+1)-choosable if it has a proper conflict-free $L$L-coloring for every such list assignment. Degree-plus-one conjecture. Every connected graph of minimum degree at least $3$3 is proper conflict-free $({\\rm degree}+1)$(degree+1)-choosable. This seeks a one-color improvement over the general degree-plus bound for graphs without vertices of degree below three, and remains open according to the source.\n\nReferences\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2025–2026). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2509.06280.\n\nSource: Masaki Kashima, Riste Škrekovski and Rongxing Xu, “Degree-choosability of proper conflict-free list coloring of sparse graphs”, arXiv:2601.15611 (2026).\n\nconflict-free-coloringgraph-coloringlist-coloring\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\nNo solutions have been posted ye